WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.668
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.668 --> 00:00:07.180
So it's a typical Thursday night.

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:12.490
As a third year, gone are the days
of beer pong Thursdays, and

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:16.440
I'm hunched over my computer in the
basement of Huang Engineering Building,

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.904
the clock ticking toward a deadline at 11.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:22.870
For the past year,
as part of my joint degree program,

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:26.800
I've been trying to teach machines
how to learn like humans.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.214
But I'm here today to tell you
about the time that machines taught

00:00:30.214 --> 00:00:32.960
me something about how to learn and
live more fully.

00:00:35.907 --> 00:00:37.265
So that day in the basement,

00:00:37.265 --> 00:00:42.260
I'm building this program that's supposed
to learn how to play blackjack on its own.

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:45.520
Using a type of machine learning
called reinforcement learning,

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.360
which is the same kind that
was used by Google's Alphago.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:54.270
But unlike Alphago, my program is
acting like a kid that never learns.

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:58.110
It keeps making the same mistakes,
like hitting on 19 or 20,

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:00.240
and sometimes standing pat on 3.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:05.915
For those of you who are not blackjack
players, those are not good moves to make.

00:01:05.915 --> 00:01:10.890
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm super frustrated, see,

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:14.430
what my program is supposed to
do is actually quite intuitive.

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:16.006
It only needs to do two things.

00:01:16.006 --> 00:01:21.680
First, it's just as any kid
would do when given a new toy.

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:26.150
It's supposed to try out a bunch
of things and collect experiences.

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:32.830
Like, try hitting on 10, or 15, or
20, and experience what happens.

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:38.260
And second, it's supposed to stop and
extract lessons from those experiences.

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:44.210
Learn things like aha, whenever I choose
hit when I'm on 20, bad things happen.

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:45.010
Not going to do that again.

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:48.990
So I spend an hour of debugging and

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:51.220
I feel like my soul just
got sucked out of my body.

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:56.660
And I realize that I've somehow built
a program that's doing a great job

00:01:56.660 --> 00:02:00.490
of collecting experiences,
running around doing all these things and

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:03.790
not stopping to learn anything
from those experiences.

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:09.090
Too busy running around to
learn anything from experience.

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:12.185
That actually didn't
sound that unfamiliar.

00:02:12.185 --> 00:02:16.200
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; As no stranger to what seems to be

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:20.340
the GSB mantra of do more,
fit more stuff in schedule and

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:24.280
be more productive, I saw a little
bit of myself in that program.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:28.860
I had to ask myself,
am I like that program, too?

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:34.070
Are we like that program that
never learns from experience?

00:02:36.937 --> 00:02:40.055
So, unfortunately, I realized yes,
I was like that program.

00:02:40.055 --> 00:02:43.330
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; More than once, for example,

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:47.580
I walked out of class thinking wow,
that speaker had such clarity of thought.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.690
I'd love to learn how to do that.

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:54.360
And I never did because I have to run to
the next BVL or the next coffee chat.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:57.880
I never took the time to think through
which of these comments struck me as

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:01.285
insightful and what it would look like
if I tried to imitate that line of

00:03:01.285 --> 00:03:02.861
thinking on a different topic.

00:03:05.837 --> 00:03:09.960
So you might be thinking, wait a second,
we're not like computers.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:15.030
Important things will stick in our memory,
even if we don't stop and reflect, right?

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:18.660
That's exactly what I was thinking, too.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:21.953
And to see if it was true, and
I really wanted it to be true,

00:03:21.953 --> 00:03:24.509
I decided to reexamine
a learning moment from

00:03:24.509 --> 00:03:29.250
my own experience to see if I truly
got out everything that I should have.

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:32.290
Knowing that I didn't have the time
to reflect on that experience.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:37.290
The experience that came to my mind was
one of my proudest moments from army

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:38.190
days in Korea.

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:40.590
Cleaning the second floor bathroom.

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:45.250
So I had just made corporal that month,
it's a pretty big deal.

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:51.575
I had this new, prestigious responsibility
as the leader of the cleaning squad.

00:03:51.575 --> 00:03:54.690
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So ten or so

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:59.060
privates from my platoon who
cleaned after work every day.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:03.030
And how we cleaned these places
never changes over time.

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:05.460
In a barrack dominated by this oppressive,

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:09.060
hierarchical culture, you simply do
what you're told by your superiors.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:14.750
But this time we really want you to
bring some fun and creativity into this.

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:18.170
So for the first couple days,
two privates walked around and

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:21.570
jotted down what everyone
was doing at any moment.

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:25.430
Then they learned to create this chart
that showed us the sequence of activities

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:30.775
that we took and where the bottlenecks
were, true consultant style.

00:04:30.775 --> 00:04:32.510
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And, with that,

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:34.790
we debated how to make our process better.

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:39.540
We'd ask things like, should we double
the personnel for wiping the toilets, so

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:42.080
whoever is watering down
doesn't have to wait?

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:48.591
Or we're using toothbrushes to clean
the urinals, not ours, but the spare ones.

00:04:48.591 --> 00:04:52.161
But the surface area is too small,
so it takes a long time to clean.

00:04:52.161 --> 00:04:53.780
How can we get normal brushes?

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:59.200
We tried having two area captains
as well as having one centralized

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:03.920
cleaning czar system, and
after a month of experiments,

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:07.290
we cut down the time from
30 minutes to 14 minutes.

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:11.250
And you might be wondering if
I'm being facetious about this

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:14.100
being one of my proud moments,
but I'm kind of not.

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:19.100
And if anybody asks,
what did you learn from that experience?

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:20.910
I'd say something like this.

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:24.260
I learned how to motivate a team,
give ownership, and

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:25.580
change an organizational culture.

00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:30.410
A fine answer, specific enough to
fool others, and most importantly,

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:33.839
myself, that I've learned
enough from that experience.

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:41.725
But if anybody further pushes, and asks
me, but really, how do you give ownership,

00:05:41.725 --> 00:05:45.620
what actionable insights did
you learn from that experience,

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:46.650
you can use here and now?

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:48.360
I'd be stumped.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:54.270
I have this log of experience in my head,
but contrary to my hopes,

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:57.420
my brain didn't magically distill
insights from that experience.

00:05:58.510 --> 00:06:02.300
Sure, some of those have popped
up in my head before, but

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:05.360
I assumed those were important
enough to stick in my memory.

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:06.650
And sure enough, they didn't.

00:06:09.311 --> 00:06:13.509
So one weekend I spent 30 minutes
to reflect on that experience.

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:19.280
And the lessons I walked away this time
were of completely different resolution.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:23.540
For example, I learned about the moment
that I become susceptible to inadvertently

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:28.520
taking ownership away from my team and
how to counteract that temptation.

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:33.590
Or the tactics I can use to address
norm violations in a stern way

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:36.470
when I'm also trying to build
a positive collaborative culture.

00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:39.230
So there it was, the verdict.

00:06:41.470 --> 00:06:45.600
The high quality insights doesn't
stick in your memory automatically.

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:47.750
I had to do the work to get there.

00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:54.500
How much of invaluable experience
you're having here at the GSB

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:55.770
are you extracting lessons from?

00:06:57.180 --> 00:06:59.552
Are you learning enough
from your experience?

00:07:03.659 --> 00:07:06.680
So why did I bring up this
complicated computer algorithm?

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:10.720
To make a simple point,
that we should stop and reflect to learn.

00:07:12.010 --> 00:07:14.199
Because it exposes an inconvenient truth.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:20.020
When dealing with fuzzy things that are
hard to quantify, like self development or

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:25.140
learning, we often to choose to believe in
mental models that are convenient to us

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:26.880
rather than the ones that
might be more accurate.

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:31.790
So when it comes to our belief about how
we learn from experience, it makes our

00:07:31.790 --> 00:07:37.850
lives so much easier to simply believe
in this mental model of aging whiskey.

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:42.540
Where, as with whiskey,
time takes care of everything, and it

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:47.070
somehow magically distills insights from
your conscious reservoir of experience.

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:53.770
But as much as I love whiskey,
I believe the fundamental

00:07:53.770 --> 00:07:59.110
relationship between experience and
learning is more accurately captured

00:07:59.110 --> 00:08:02.806
by the mental model of that
dumb blackjack program.

00:08:02.806 --> 00:08:07.100
If we don't stop and
reflect, we will not learn.

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:13.520
We have to put in the work to
get what's important to us.

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:17.630
Because no one else, not time,
not our subconscious,

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:18.949
is going to do that job for us.

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:23.760
So I hope this program, this dumb program

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:28.160
could act as the same wake up call as
it did to me, and motivates you to

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:32.240
spend five minutes every day to make
sense of what happened that day.

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:35.753
I hope we can all live and

00:08:35.753 --> 00:08:40.454
learn to be a little better
today than we were yesterday.

00:08:40.454 --> 00:08:44.724
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:44.724 --> 00:08:48.869
[MUSIC]

